# Argus — Release Manifest

- **Agent:** Argus
- **Version:** 0.1.0
- **Code name:** Panoptes

## Code name origin

**Panoptes** (Ancient Greek: Πανόπτης, *Panoptēs* — "the All-Seeing") was the
hundred-eyed giant of Greek myth. With eyes covering his entire body, he
never had to fully sleep: while some eyes rested, others stayed open, so
nothing ever escaped his watch. Hera set him to guard the heifer Io, and
not even Hermes, messenger of the gods, could slip past him unnoticed —
he had to lull *every single eye* shut first.

Argus Panoptes is the namesake of the word *panoptic* — seeing everything.

**Every release of Argus takes the code name of a watcher from myth and
legend.** v0.1.0 starts at the source: **Panoptes, the original all-seeing
process monitor** — the first daemon that never sleeps. The alpha builds
(`0.1.0-alpha.N`) were the private testing loop that burned the issues down
before this cut; they share the code name with the release they lead to, and
the name changes when the minor version does.

For devs: an agent that reads your screen tree, watches your windows, drives
your browser, and keeps working while you look away needs exactly one property
above all others — it must never lose track of what it is looking at. That is
the whole of Panoptes, and it is why the name starts here.

## Status

**0.1.0 is the release.** The `0.1.0-alpha.N` builds were the testing loop —
prereleases for people installing the app to find what was broken while it was
still burning. They are signed with self-signed certificates, so every platform
showed a warning on first launch; that was expected and was not evidence of a
tampered download. This build is signed with a real certificate issued to
**Anyscape Labs PLC**.

## Features in Panoptes (v0.1.0)

This is the first release of Argus — there is no earlier release to diff
against, so everything below is new.

- **Agent chat** — persistent sessions, `ask`/`never` permission modes,
  streaming tool output, compacting summaries.
- **Computer control** — terminal-first: streaming shell with cwd and
  configurable timeouts, recursive grep with file globs, files, processes,
  installs, and scripts.
- **In-place document editing** — `doc.edit` rewrites a library document from
  full content in place (same id, same kind, new bytes); `doc.patch` makes
  token-light `SEARCH/REPLACE` fixes that fail atomically if an `old` string
  does not match exactly once, so a patch can never silently corrupt a file.
- **Browser use** — snapshot-ref interaction with stale-reference recovery,
  via extension or isolated profiles. The Chrome extension is installed
  separately from the app (see `extension/`).
- **Skills & memory** — reusable skills, durable fact/preference memory.
- **Document library** — docx, pdf, pptx, xlsx, csv, md, txt generation.
- **Model providers** — Anthropic + any OpenAI-compatible endpoint
  (OpenAI, Baseten, self-hosted Ollama); keys in the OS keyring.
- **Fourteen integrations** — Google Workspace, GitHub, GitLab, Slack,
  Notion, Linear, Outlook, Discord, Figma, Home Assistant, Spotify,
  Telegram, Todoist, Trello.
- **Sandbox** — OS-level confinement (Landlock, seccomp, rlimits) for terminal
  execution, failing closed when a policy cannot be enforced.
- **Local-first** — SQLite + OS keyring; your data stays on your machine.

### How it is built and shipped

- **Signed release builds for every platform** — macOS (both architectures),
  Windows, and Linux. See `docs/developer-guide/releasing.md`.
- **CI on every push and pull request** — format, clippy with `-D warnings`, the
  full backend suite, typecheck, and a production frontend build.

### Not in this build

- **Voice input.** Local dictation was built and removed before the first
  alpha. A transcript is indistinguishable from typing, so a misheard
  instruction becomes an executed one, and the read-back half wanted cloud
  text-to-speech — which breaks the offline promise. Possible later, not
  something this build claims.